-- Q1652: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Considered the most reliable method for statistical sampling","Required whenever transactions follow a cyclical pattern","Preferred over random selection when populations are large","Acceptable only when the auditor can demonstrate that selected blocks are representative"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Block (cluster) selection involves choosing contiguous items, such as all transactions in a specific time period. Under AU-C 530, this method is generally discouraged because a few blocks may not represent the entire population. It is acceptable only when the auditor can justify representativeness.
Wrong (A): Block selection is considered less reliable than random or systematic selection for statistical sampling purposes.
Wrong (B): No auditing standard requires block selection for cyclical transactions; random or systematic methods are preferred.
Wrong (C): Block selection is not preferred over random selection regardless of population size.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1652;

-- Q1656: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Determine the exact value of the population being tested","Calculate the appropriate audit fee for the engagement","Identify each individual misstatement within the population","Estimate a range likely to contain the true population value at a given confidence level"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In classical variables sampling under AU-C 530, the confidence interval provides a range within which the true population value is expected to fall at a specified confidence level. The auditor compares this range to the recorded amount and tolerable misstatement to assess potential material misstatement.
Wrong (A): Sampling provides an estimate, not an exact population value; absolute precision would require 100% testing.
Wrong (B): Confidence intervals relate to population estimation, not audit fee determination.
Wrong (C): The confidence interval estimates total population characteristics, not individual item misstatements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1656;

-- Q1657: Audit Sampling [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stratified sampling with complete examination of the top stratum","Block selection applied to high-value receivable balances","Haphazard sampling with a judgmental dollar threshold","Attribute sampling focused on large monetary amounts"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530, testing 100% of items above a threshold and sampling the remainder is stratified sampling. The top stratum receives complete examination because large items present the greatest dollar exposure, while sampling the rest achieves efficiency.
Wrong (B): Block selection involves choosing contiguous items from a sequence, not items above a dollar threshold.
Wrong (C): Haphazard sampling involves selecting items without a structured pattern, not threshold-based testing.
Wrong (D): Attribute sampling tests rates of deviation in controls, not monetary amounts in account balances.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1657;

-- Q1658: Audit Sampling [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor is not required to define a specific sampling objective","Sample results cannot support conclusions about the tested population","The same conceptual factors apply even though they are not mathematically quantified","Nonstatistical sampling always produces a smaller sample than statistical methods"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, nonstatistical sampling uses professional judgment rather than formulas, but the auditor must still consider tolerable rate, expected deviation rate, and desired assurance level. These factors drive sample size decisions regardless of the sampling approach used.
Wrong (A): Both statistical and nonstatistical sampling require a clearly defined sampling objective before selection begins.
Wrong (B): Nonstatistical sampling results can and should support conclusions about the population when properly applied.
Wrong (D): Nonstatistical sampling does not inherently require smaller samples; sample size depends on the auditor''s judgment of the same risk factors.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1658;

-- Q1659: Audit Sampling [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The planned reliance on the control is fully supported by the results","The sample size selected was too large for the test objective","The control cannot be relied upon, requiring upward revision of control risk","The tolerable deviation rate should be reduced and the control retested"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 530, when the achieved upper deviation rate (7%) exceeds the tolerable deviation rate (5%), sample results do not support planned reliance on the control. The auditor must increase assessed control risk and plan additional substantive procedures to compensate.
Wrong (A): An upper deviation rate exceeding the tolerable rate means the results contradict planned reliance, not support it.
Wrong (B): The issue is the deviation rate exceeding tolerance, not that the sample was oversized.
Wrong (D): Reducing the tolerable rate would make the gap worse, not resolve the control reliance problem.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1659;

-- Q1662: Audit Sampling [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sample size increases because the gap between expected and tolerable rates narrows","Sample size decreases because a higher expected rate reduces required precision","Sample size remains unchanged because the tolerable rate is held constant","The auditor should abandon attribute sampling and switch to variables sampling"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 530, when the expected population deviation rate rises from 1.5% to 3% while the tolerable rate stays at 6%, the gap narrows from 4.5% to 3%. A larger sample is needed to determine with the same confidence whether the true deviation rate exceeds the tolerable rate.
Wrong (B): A higher expected deviation rate requires a larger sample, not a smaller one, to maintain the same precision.
Wrong (C): Sample size is affected by both the expected and tolerable rates, not just the tolerable rate alone.
Wrong (D): Nothing about a change in expected deviation rate requires switching sampling methods.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1662;

-- Q1673: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Issue a disclaimer because this scope limitation is always considered material","Perform alternative procedures such as observing a subsequent count and testing intervening transactions","Accept management''s inventory count as sufficient audit evidence without further testing","Resign from the engagement due to the inability to observe the count"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 501.05, when the auditor cannot attend the physical count, alternative procedures should be performed. These include observing a subsequent count, testing rollback or rollforward of transactions, and inspecting purchase and sales records. A scope limitation applies only if alternatives are insufficient.
Wrong (A): Issuing a disclaimer without first attempting alternative procedures does not satisfy AU-C 501 requirements.
Wrong (C): Accepting management''s count without corroboration fails to provide sufficient appropriate audit evidence.
Wrong (D): Resignation is premature when alternative procedures may provide the necessary evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1673;

-- Q1677: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A modest revenue decline of 5% compared to the prior fiscal year","A change in depreciation method from straight-line to double-declining balance","Debt covenant violations combined with inability to obtain financing and recurring operating losses","A minor pending lawsuit with an estimated probable loss of only ten thousand dollars"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 570.A3, substantial doubt about going concern arises from conditions such as recurring operating losses, debt covenant violations, inability to obtain financing, and depletion of working capital. Multiple adverse indicators together strongly suggest the entity may not meet its obligations.
Wrong (A): A 5% revenue decline alone, without other adverse indicators, is unlikely to raise substantial doubt about going concern.
Wrong (B): Changing a depreciation method is an accounting policy choice, not an indicator of financial distress.
Wrong (D): A minor lawsuit with a small probable loss does not threaten the entity''s ability to continue operations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1677;

-- Q1680: Substantive Procedures [easy/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Pending or threatened litigation, claims, and assessments including likelihood and potential loss range","The entity''s overall financial health and long-term viability as a business","The design adequacy of the entity''s internal control environment","Whether the entity''s federal and state tax returns were filed by their deadlines"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 501.12, the inquiry letter to legal counsel requests evaluation of pending or threatened litigation, claims, and assessments. The attorney evaluates the likelihood of unfavorable outcomes consistent with ASC 450 and provides loss estimates if reasonably estimable.
Wrong (B): The letter addresses specific legal matters, not general assessments of the entity''s overall financial condition.
Wrong (C): Evaluating internal controls is the auditor''s responsibility, not something requested from legal counsel.
Wrong (D): Tax filing compliance is not within the scope of the standard legal inquiry letter.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1680;

-- Q1684: Substantive Procedures [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Require the client to conduct a complete physical count at year-end regardless of system reliability","Accept the perpetual inventory records as sufficient evidence without performing any testing","Evaluate the perpetual system''s reliability, observe selected cycle counts, and test the roll-forward","Issue a disclaimer of opinion because no single year-end physical count was performed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 501.A14, when a client uses perpetual inventory with cycle counts, the auditor evaluates the perpetual system''s reliability, observes selected cycle counts, and tests the roll-forward to year-end. A full year-end count is not required when the system is reliable.
Wrong (A): Requiring a full year-end count when the perpetual system is reliable would be unnecessarily burdensome.
Wrong (B): Accepting perpetual records without testing would leave the existence and accuracy assertions unverified.
Wrong (D): A disclaimer is unwarranted when alternative procedures through cycle count observation are available.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1684;

-- Q1686: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only the client''s trial balance and general ledger detail","The prior year''s audit report and management representation letter","The client''s marketing materials and public advertising","Proxy statements, SEC filings, organizational charts, and management inquiries about affiliates"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 550.12, related parties are identified by reviewing proxy statements, SEC filings, corporate minutes, organizational charts, and conflict-of-interest questionnaires, and by inquiring of management about relationships with officers, directors, and affiliates.
Wrong (A): The trial balance alone does not reveal related party relationships that are not evident from transaction descriptions.
Wrong (B): The prior year audit report may identify previously known parties but would miss newly formed relationships.
Wrong (C): Marketing materials are not a reliable source for identifying related party relationships.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1686;

-- Q1687: Substantive Procedures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Whether the audit fee for the engagement has already been collected from the client","Whether the client has specifically requested the auditor to investigate the matter","Whether the applicable statute of limitations on auditor liability has expired","Whether the statements have been issued and whether the fact would have affected the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 560.14-.17, when the auditor discovers a fact that existed at the report date, the response depends on whether the financial statements have been issued to users and whether the fact would have affected the report. Pre-issuance and post-issuance situations require different actions.
Wrong (A): Fee collection status has no bearing on the auditor''s professional obligations regarding subsequently discovered facts.
Wrong (B): The auditor''s responsibility to act exists regardless of whether the client requests an investigation.
Wrong (C): The statute of limitations does not determine the auditor''s professional response to subsequently discovered facts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1687;

-- Q1692: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always identify the specialist by name in the body of the audit report","Require the specialist to cosign the audit report as a co-author","Disclaim all responsibility for the conclusions reached by the specialist","Omit reference to the specialist in the report when the opinion is unmodified"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620.15, when issuing an unmodified opinion, the auditor should not refer to the auditor''s specialist in the report. Such reference could be misunderstood as a qualification or division of responsibility. Reference is permitted only when explaining a modified opinion.
Wrong (A): Naming the specialist in an unmodified report is inappropriate because it may imply divided responsibility.
Wrong (B): The specialist does not sign the audit report; the auditor retains sole responsibility for the opinion.
Wrong (C): The auditor cannot disclaim responsibility for the specialist''s work; the auditor must evaluate and take responsibility for it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1692;

-- Q1693: Substantive Procedures [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor selects items, sends requests directly, and receives responses without client interception","The auditor reviews confirmation responses only after management sends them out","Management sends the confirmations and forwards copies of all responses to the auditor","The client''s internal audit department manages the entire confirmation mailing process"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 505.08-.10, maintaining control means the auditor selects confirmation items, prepares or supervises preparation of requests, sends them directly, and ensures responses come back to the auditor without client interception.
Wrong (B): Reviewing responses after management sends them does not constitute maintaining control over the process.
Wrong (C): Allowing management to send confirmations compromises evidence integrity because responses could be intercepted or altered.
Wrong (D): Delegating the process to internal audit does not satisfy the external auditor''s control requirement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1693;

-- Q1700: Substantive Procedures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Review only standard recurring journal entries to confirm they are recorded completely","Accept management''s verbal assurance that all journal entries reflect legitimate transactions","Test non-standard entries made near period-end or to unusual accounts for supporting documentation","Test only journal entries that individually exceed a predetermined materiality dollar threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 240.32, when fraud risk involving journal entries exists, the auditor should test non-standard entries, particularly those made near period-end, posted to unusual accounts, or prepared by senior personnel outside normal duties. Supporting documentation and business purpose should be examined.
Wrong (A): Focusing only on standard recurring entries would miss the unusual entries most likely to be used for manipulation.
Wrong (B): Accepting management''s explanation without corroboration is insufficient when fraud risk has been identified.
Wrong (D): Using only a dollar threshold would miss smaller entries that could collectively be used to manipulate results.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1700;

-- Q1703: Substantive Procedures [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the January recording date because the invoices were ultimately paid on time","Notify the SEC immediately about the late recording of these payable amounts","Propose an adjusting entry to record the payables in December and evaluate materiality","Disregard the timing difference if individual invoice amounts fall below the significant threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Invoices for goods or services received before year-end that were recorded in January represent a cutoff error understating accounts payable and overstating net income. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should propose an adjusting entry and evaluate whether the aggregate misstatement is material.
Wrong (A): Timely payment does not excuse failure to record the liability in the correct period.
Wrong (B): Cutoff errors are addressed through adjusting entries, not regulatory notification.
Wrong (D): Cutoff misstatements must be aggregated with other misstatements; individual insignificance does not justify ignoring them.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1703;

-- Q1708: Substantive Procedures [medium/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept management''s assertion that all transactions were conducted at arm''s length pricing","Disregard the transactions because spousal relationships fall outside related party definitions","Examine transaction terms, compare pricing to market rates, and verify required disclosures","Require the CEO to divest the spouse''s ownership interest before completing the audit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 550.20, the auditor evaluates related party transactions by examining terms and conditions, comparing them to arm''s-length market rates, assessing economic substance and business purpose, and verifying that all required disclosures are included in the financial statements.
Wrong (A): Simply accepting management''s assertion without independent evaluation would not provide sufficient appropriate evidence.
Wrong (B): Spousal relationships are within the definition of related parties under ASC 850 and AU-C 550.
Wrong (D): The auditor has no authority to require divestiture of ownership interests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1708;

-- Q1710: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A statement that the auditor has verified all transactions during the reporting period","An addressee identifying the intended recipients of the audit report","A report title that includes the word independent to convey objectivity","The signature of the auditor or the audit firm issuing the report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The auditor''s report does not state that all transactions were verified. Under AU-C 700, an audit provides reasonable assurance, not absolute verification of every transaction. Such a statement would overstate the scope of the engagement.
Wrong (B): The addressee is a required element of the standard audit report under AU-C 700.
Wrong (C): AU-C 700 requires the title to include the word "independent" to communicate the auditor''s objectivity.
Wrong (D): The auditor''s signature is a required element of the standard audit report under AU-C 700.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1710;

-- Q1721: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The specific audit procedures performed along with their detailed quantitative results","The dollar amount of each individual misstatement the audit team identified","Why the matter was considered a key audit matter and how it was addressed","Management''s formal written response to each identified significant audit risk"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 701.13, for each key audit matter the auditor must describe why the matter was considered a key audit matter and how it was addressed in the audit. This communicates significance and the auditor''s response without providing detailed procedure results.
Wrong (A): KAM disclosures describe how the matter was addressed, not the detailed results of specific procedures.
Wrong (B): Specific misstatement dollar amounts are not included in key audit matter disclosures.
Wrong (D): Management''s response to risks is not a required element of the KAM description.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1721;

-- Q1725: Audit Reports [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Withdraw from the engagement immediately without further discussion or communication","Issue an adverse opinion on the audited financial statements due to the inconsistency","Communicate the matter to those charged with governance and consider report implications","Take no further action because the other information falls outside the audit scope"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 720.12, when a material inconsistency exists and management refuses to revise, the auditor communicates the matter to those charged with governance. AU-C 720.13 requires the auditor to consider including an other-matter paragraph or, in serious cases, withdrawing.
Wrong (A): Immediate withdrawal without first communicating to governance would bypass required procedures under AU-C 720.
Wrong (B): A material inconsistency in other information does not automatically warrant an adverse opinion on the financial statements.
Wrong (D): AU-C 720 imposes affirmative responsibilities on the auditor regarding material inconsistencies in other information.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1725;

-- Q1733: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Immediately issue a disclaimer of opinion on the financial statements","Request removal of the limitation and communicate with those charged with governance","Withdraw from the engagement without any further communication to the client","Issue an unmodified opinion and document the limitation only in the workpapers"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 705, when management imposes a scope limitation, the auditor should first request removal. If management refuses, the auditor communicates with those charged with governance and determines whether alternative procedures can provide sufficient evidence before deciding on opinion modification.
Wrong (A): Issuing a disclaimer is premature before requesting removal of the limitation and exploring alternatives.
Wrong (C): Withdrawal without communication to governance is not the required first response under AU-C 705.
Wrong (D): Issuing an unmodified opinion when a material scope limitation exists would violate professional standards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1733;

-- Q1736: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Only a general statement indicating that the financial statements contain a misstatement","A recommendation describing how management should correct the identified departure","A reference to the specific tax code section that applies to the GAAP departure","A description of the matter and quantification of its financial effects if practicable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, the Basis for Qualified Opinion section must describe the matter giving rise to the modification. For misstatements, the auditor quantifies the financial effects if practicable. For scope limitations, the section explains why sufficient evidence could not be obtained.
Wrong (A): A general statement alone is insufficient; the specific matter causing the qualification must be described.
Wrong (B): The auditor does not make recommendations for corrective action in the Basis for Qualified Opinion section.
Wrong (C): The tax code is not referenced in this section; the focus is on the departure from the financial reporting framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1736;

-- Q1746: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["International Financial Reporting Standards as issued by the IASB","Auditing standards generally accepted in the United States of America","Generally accepted accounting principles as the applicable reporting framework","Internal auditing standards established by the Institute of Internal Auditors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 700, the Basis for Opinion section states that the audit was conducted in accordance with auditing standards generally accepted in the United States of America (GAAS). These standards govern how the audit is performed and the evidence obtained.
Wrong (A): IFRS is a financial reporting framework used to prepare financial statements, not an auditing standard.
Wrong (C): GAAP is the framework against which financial statements are evaluated, not the standard under which the audit is conducted.
Wrong (D): Internal auditing standards govern internal audit functions, not external financial statement audits.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1746;

-- Q1749: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An unmodified opinion because disclosures are supplementary to the statements","An adverse opinion because any GAAP departure requires the strongest modification","A disclaimer of opinion due to insufficient evidence about the omitted disclosure","A qualified opinion because the departure is material but not pervasive"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 705, a missing required GAAP disclosure constitutes a departure from the financial reporting framework. When the omission is material but not pervasive, a qualified opinion is appropriate, and the Basis for Qualified Opinion section describes the omitted disclosure.
Wrong (A): A material disclosure omission requires opinion modification; an unmodified opinion would be inappropriate.
Wrong (B): An adverse opinion is reserved for departures that are both material and pervasive to the financial statements.
Wrong (C): A disclaimer relates to scope limitations, not departures from the reporting framework.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1749;

-- Q1750: Audit Reports [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about continued operations for a reasonable period","Guarantee that the entity will be able to continue as a going concern indefinitely","Predict the entity''s future profitability and long-term revenue trajectory","Determine whether the entity should file for bankruptcy protection with the court"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AU-C 570.10, the auditor''s responsibility is to evaluate whether substantial doubt exists about the entity''s ability to continue as a going concern for a reasonable period, defined as one year from the date the financial statements are issued or available to be issued.
Wrong (B): The auditor provides no guarantee of continued viability; audits offer reasonable assurance, not absolute certainty.
Wrong (C): Predicting future profitability is a management function, not an auditor obligation under auditing standards.
Wrong (D): Recommending bankruptcy is a management and legal decision outside the auditor''s professional responsibilities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1750;

-- Q1754: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Comparing recorded amounts to physical counts of the client''s inventory items","Evaluating financial data through analysis of plausible relationships among financial and nonfinancial information","Confirming material account balances directly with independent third parties","Testing the operating effectiveness of the entity''s internal controls over reporting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AR-C 90.24, analytical procedures in a review involve evaluating financial information by studying plausible relationships among financial and nonfinancial data. This includes comparing current-year amounts to prior years, industry data, and expected relationships.
Wrong (A): Physical counts are audit procedures under AU-C standards that exceed the scope of a review engagement.
Wrong (C): Third-party confirmations are audit-level procedures, not part of review engagement requirements under AR-C 90.
Wrong (D): Testing internal controls is an audit procedure and is not performed in a review engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1754;

-- Q1755: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Verify the accuracy of all underlying accounting records before presenting statements","Confirm all material account balances with independent third-party sources","Perform analytical procedures on every material account in the financial statements","Read the compiled statements and consider whether they are free from obvious material errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 80.17, in a compilation the accountant reads the financial statements and considers whether they appear appropriate in form and free from obvious material misstatements. No assurance is provided, and no verification, confirmation, or analytical procedures are required.
Wrong (A): Verifying underlying records exceeds compilation scope and would constitute review or audit-level work.
Wrong (B): Confirming balances with third parties is an audit procedure not required in a compilation engagement.
Wrong (C): Analytical procedures are required in reviews under AR-C 90, not in compilations under AR-C 80.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1755;

-- Q1757: Review and Compilation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Prepares or assists in preparing financial statements without being required to issue a report","Must issue a report that expresses limited assurance on the financial statements","Must be independent of the entity before accepting the preparation engagement","Must verify the accuracy of the trial balance before preparing the statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under AR-C 70, a preparation engagement involves the accountant assisting management in preparing financial statements without issuing a report and without providing any assurance. Independence is not required for preparation engagements.
Wrong (B): Limited assurance is provided in review engagements under AR-C 90, not in preparation engagements.
Wrong (C): Independence is not required for preparation engagements under AR-C 70.
Wrong (D): The accountant is not required to verify the trial balance in a preparation engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1757;

-- Q1758: Review and Compilation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, independence is always required before performing any compilation engagement","Yes, unless the entity qualifies as a nonprofit organization under SSARS","No, independence is never relevant and need not be considered for compilations","No, but the lack of independence must be disclosed in the compilation report"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 80.24, independence is not required for compilations. However, if the accountant is not independent, the compilation report must include a statement disclosing the lack of independence. The reason for impairment need not be disclosed unless the accountant chooses to do so.
Wrong (A): Independence is required for reviews under AR-C 90 but not for compilations under AR-C 80.
Wrong (B): The nonprofit status of the entity has no bearing on independence requirements for compilation engagements.
Wrong (C): Independence is relevant to compilations because its absence must be disclosed, even though it is not required.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1758;

-- Q1763: Review and Compilation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["We have audited the accompanying financial statements in accordance with GAAS","We have verified all transactions and account balances presented in the statements","The financial statements are guaranteed to be completely free from material misstatement","We are not aware of any material modifications needed for conformity with the framework"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AR-C 90, the standard review report includes the limited (negative) assurance conclusion stating the accountant is not aware of any material modifications that should be made for the statements to conform with the applicable framework. This language is the hallmark of a review.
Wrong (A): "We have audited" is audit report language under AU-C 700 and is not used in review reports.
Wrong (B): Verification of all transactions implies absolute assurance, which no engagement level provides.
Wrong (C): No engagement provides a guarantee of freedom from material misstatement; reviews provide only limited assurance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1763;
